\chapter{Case Study V: A Binary Protocol
Toolchain}
\label{ch:case-binary}\label{c42-case-study-v-a-binary-protocol-toolchain}

\section{The problem: a record format and a transport are different
layers}\label{c42-the-problem-a-record-format-and-a-transport-are-different-layers}

The final case study constructs a small binary protocol toolchain. A
frame contains an ASCII-like identifier such as \code{sensor_7}. The
encoder writes a magic value, version, length, payload, and checksum.
The decoder validates the record and returns an owned \code{Frame}. A
deliberately short-transfer backend exercises the I/O adapter's
completion loops.

DSLtk provides \code{BinaryStream}, explicit-endian integer operations,
\code{StaticRegex}, codec adapters, and byte I/O adapters. The
application defines the wire format, length bound, identifier
vocabulary, checksum, and decode transaction. No single toolkit type
supplies the whole protocol. The interesting work is composing the
boundaries so a complete transfer does not get confused with a valid
frame.

The example uses an in-memory backend to make every transfer
deterministic. Reads return at most two bytes and writes at most three.
Even a fourteen-byte frame therefore requires several operations. This
is more revealing than a backend that always completes the entire
requested span in one call, because it tests the partial-progress
behavior that the adapter is intended to handle.

\section{The wire format}\label{c42-the-wire-format}

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Field & Width & Encoding \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
Magic & Two bytes & ASCII \code{D}, \code{T} \\
Version & One byte & Unsigned value 1 \\
Payload length & Two bytes & Unsigned big endian \\
Payload & Declared length & Lowercase identifier bytes \\
Checksum & One byte & Sum of payload bytes modulo 256 \\
\end{longtable}
}

The payload must match \texttt{{[}a-z{]}{[}a-z0-9\_{]}*} and contain at
most sixty-four bytes. The regex requires a nonempty identifier
beginning with a lowercase letter. The format uses bytes, not Unicode
characters. An application needing Unicode identifiers would need a
different vocabulary and length policy, including a decision about
whether its length counts bytes or decoded code points.

The checksum is a small accidental-corruption check chosen for
transparency. It is not an authentication mechanism, and the case study
makes no security claim about it. Its role here is to demonstrate a
trailer that must be validated after the payload has been read. A
stronger checksum or authenticated format could replace it without
changing the adapter layering.

\section{Encoding validates before it
emits}\label{c42-encoding-validates-before-it-emits}

The codec first checks the payload length and whole-string regex. Only
then does it write the magic, version, big-endian length, payload bytes,
and checksum. This ordering avoids emitting a partial record for a
payload that is already known to violate the format. A complex encoder
could instead stage an entire record and publish it after all validation
succeeds.

The length is cast to a sixteen-bit integer only after checking the much
smaller sixty-four-byte bound. The bound therefore justifies the
conversion. Writing the integer with explicit big endian makes the wire
representation independent of the host machine's byte order. The payload
itself is a byte view of the string and is appended to the owned stream
buffer.

\code{BinaryStream::write} appends; it does not write at the read
cursor. The encoder starts with an empty stream, so this behavior
naturally constructs the complete record. The cursor remains available
for a subsequent decoding pass over the same buffer. Treating the object
as a conventional seekable file with one read/write position would lead
to different expectations.

\section{Decoding is a cursor
transaction}\label{c42-decoding-is-a-cursor-transaction}

The decoder saves the input cursor before reading any field. It checks
the magic and version, reads the declared length, enforces the bound,
reads the payload, validates the identifier, and checks the checksum.
Only after all checks succeed does it return the decoded frame. On any
exception it restores the saved cursor and rethrows.

This rollback policy is possible because the input is already buffered
in a \code{BinaryStream}. A nonseekable external transport could not
generally undo consumed bytes without retaining them. The application
deliberately separates transport loading from record decoding so the
decoder's transactional contract is simple and testable.

Restoring the cursor does not mean all arbitrary side effects are rolled
back. The decoder in this case only creates local values and reads the
buffer, so cursor restoration is enough. A decoder that updated a global
registry before checksum validation would need a broader transaction
policy. As in the configuration case study, delayed publication is
easier to reason about than undoing every intermediate side effect.

\section{Borrowed spans become an owned
payload}\label{c42-borrowed-spans-become-an-owned-payload}

\texttt{read(length)} returns a view into the stream's byte vector. The
decoder constructs an owned string from that view before returning. The
resulting \code{Frame} does not depend on the input stream remaining
alive or unchanged. The view is used only while the buffer is stable
during decoding.

This copying decision is intentional. A zero-copy frame type could
retain an owner for the input buffer and store a span or offset range,
but that would change the frame's lifetime contract. For a payload
capped at sixty-four bytes, the simple owned representation is easy to
audit. Optimization should follow evidence that this copy matters rather
than silently introducing borrowed state into an apparently
self-contained result.

The checksum iterates through unsigned byte values, avoiding
signed-character interpretation. The accumulator is reduced modulo 256
when producing the eight-bit checksum. The payload vocabulary is
ASCII-like, but explicit unsigned treatment still documents the intended
byte arithmetic.

\section{Codec adapters preserve the application
format}\label{c42-codec-adapters-preserve-the-application-format}

The \code{Codec} object provides \code{serialize} and a templated
\texttt{deserialize\textless{}T\textgreater{}}. The deserializer
statically restricts \code{T} to the application \code{Frame} type.
\code{dsl::Serializer} and \code{dsl::Deserializer} borrow this object
and forward the operations. They do not add a second framing layer or
choose a hidden binary encoding for the C++ struct.

This demonstrates what the generic codec protocol actually buys: a
consistent adapter interface around user-defined semantics. A different
frame type or version can have another codec while using the same byte
stream and transport completion mechanisms. The protocol's format
remains visible in application code rather than inferred from memory
layout or reflection.

The codec object outlives both adapters in \code{main}. The adapters are
local borrowers, and all calls finish before the codec is destroyed.
This is a small ownership fact, but making it obvious is preferable to
returning an adapter that refers to a temporary backend.

\section{Partial transfers exercise the real I/O
contract}\label{c42-partial-transfers-exercise-the-real-io-contract}

The \code{ShortBuffer} backend stores bytes and a read cursor. Its write
operation appends at most three bytes from the requested span. Its read
operation copies at most two available bytes into the destination and
advances its cursor. When no input remains, it returns zero, which the
I/O protocol interprets as EOF.

\texttt{encoded.save(io)} uses write-all, so the short writes must
eventually transfer the complete record.
\texttt{BinaryStream::load(io,1024)} repeatedly reads until EOF and
enforces an overall input bound of 1024 bytes. That buffer limit is
separate from the codec's sixty-four-byte payload limit. One constrains
the loaded artifact; the other constrains a field in each frame.

The backend's read cursor remains within its stored data, so
remaining-size subtraction is well defined. A production backend with a
public seek method would need to preserve or check that invariant as
well. The adapter validates returned counts, but it cannot prove that
the backend copied the correct bytes. The round-trip equality check
supplies evidence for the combined path.

\section{The complete implementation}\label{c42-the-complete-implementation}

The source is \code{projects/binary/main.cpp}. Build it with
\code{make build/binary}. The program is also part of the aggregate
examples target and includes rejection tests for every truncated prefix
and for a corrupted checksum.

\lstinputlisting[style=dslcpp,caption={Case study V: an explicit binary frame codec over short-transfer I/O.},label={lst:case-binary}]{projects/binary/main.cpp}

The observed output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Notation, algorithm, or output}]
frame=sensor_7 bytes=14 partial-I/O round trip OK
\end{lstlisting}

For \code{sensor_7}, the payload length is eight and the checksum is
decimal forty-eight, hexadecimal 30. The full frame bytes are:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Notation, algorithm, or output}]
44 54 01 00 08 73 65 6e 73 6f 72 5f 37 30
\end{lstlisting}

That explicit byte sequence is an independent description of the format.
It checks the two-byte magic, version, big-endian length, payload
spelling, and trailer. A successful encoder-decoder round trip alone
would not detect a mutually consistent but incorrectly little-endian
implementation.

\section{Truncation tests establish a strong boundary
property}\label{c42-truncation-tests-establish-a-strong-boundary-property}

The program creates every proper prefix of the encoded record, from zero
bytes through one byte short of completion. Each prefix must be rejected
by the decoder, and the cursor must return to its original zero
position. This tests header, length, payload, and checksum truncation
without needing a separate hand-written case for every field boundary.

The test is meaningful because the frame format requires all of those
bytes for acceptance. A decoder that accidentally accepted a missing
checksum or used an unchecked span would violate the property. Checking
cursor restoration also verifies the transactional contract, not merely
that an exception was thrown somewhere.

The checksum corruption test copies the complete encoded stream, flips
one bit of the trailer, and requires an invalid-argument rejection with
the cursor restored. This distinguishes complete malformed input from
incomplete input. The current API reports both through exceptions, but
the tests retain their conceptual difference.

\section{Multiple records and incremental
transport}\label{c42-multiple-records-and-incremental-transport}

The decoder consumes one frame and leaves the cursor after it. The
example checks EOF because its input contains exactly one record. A
multi-record consumer could call the decoder again while bytes remain,
provided it has a policy for trailing malformed or incomplete data. The
codec itself should not silently discard a suffix merely because one
valid frame was found.

For an incremental network stream, incomplete input may mean more bytes
will arrive rather than final truncation. A production streaming decoder
could return a distinct need-more-input status and preserve the buffered
prefix. The current count-based I/O adapter treats zero read as EOF, so
a nonblocking would-block condition needs another representation or
driver policy. The buffered example deliberately avoids conflating these
cases.

Versioning also needs an explicit policy. Version one is the only
accepted value here. A future version can change field meanings only if
the decoder knows how to distinguish and interpret it. Unknown versions
should be rejected or retained according to a documented forwarding
policy, not interpreted opportunistically as version one because the
first few fields happen to look familiar.

\section{What the five studies establish
together}\label{c42-what-the-five-studies-establish-together}

The units study separated lexical conversion from dimensional meaning.
The query study separated generic rewriting from semantic laws.
Configuration separated recognition from all-or-nothing publication.
Saturation separated known equality, resource limits, and extraction
cost. Binary framing now separates format validation, cursor ownership,
and transport completion.

These are the recurring design decisions behind DSL construction. DSLtk
supplies compact representations and algorithms, while the application
must state what values mean, who owns them, how failure is represented,
and what evidence supports an optimization or conversion. The primary
C++ header and secondary D module express those mechanisms differently,
and the book has treated those differences as implementation facts
rather than smoothing them away behind feature names.

The final artifact is therefore more than a collection of syntax
examples. It is a method for reading and using a small language toolkit:
follow the actual implementation, separate static information from
runtime work, preserve domain distinctions in representations, and
verify the boundaries that connect one stage to the next. Those habits
remain useful when the language grows beyond the modest case studies
presented here.

\section{Wire compatibility as a separate test
oracle}\label{c42-wire-compatibility-as-a-separate-test-oracle}

The expected fourteen bytes give the encoder a reference independent of
its decoder. A compatibility test can compare each produced byte with
that sequence and report the first differing offset. The reverse test
feeds the known bytes directly to the decoder without first invoking the
encoder. Together they check both directions against a stated format
instead of merely checking that two pieces of application code share the
same mistake.

The length field is especially important because this host may be little
endian while the format is big endian. For an eight-byte payload, the
two length bytes must be \code{00 08}. A length of eight is large enough
to distinguish the ordering and small enough to make the frame easy to
inspect. Additional tests at the sixty-four-byte boundary exercise the
application limit, while sixty-five bytes should be rejected before
encoding and after reading a declared length during decoding.

Magic and version tests should modify the corresponding header bytes in
an otherwise valid frame. This isolates the reason for rejection. A
checksum test should similarly leave the header and payload length
intact. Random corruption can be useful, but a collection of targeted
mutations explains which validation obligations the decoder actually
enforces and makes a regression easier to diagnose.

The decoder's rollback contract can also be tested from a nonzero
cursor. Place a valid prefix record or unrelated bytes before the
candidate frame, seek to the candidate's beginning, and attempt a
failing decode. The cursor must return to that saved beginning rather
than to absolute zero. The implementation saves its starting cursor, and
the current truncation loop tests the zero-position instance of that
more general property.

\section{Separating record limits from transport
limits}\label{c42-separating-record-limits-from-transport-limits}

The stream loader's 1024-byte bound limits the entire buffered input.
The codec's sixty-four-byte bound limits one payload. With multiple
records, an input can contain individually valid frames yet exceed the
overall buffer budget. Conversely, a small input can declare an
excessive payload length and should be rejected by the codec even though
the loader's bound was not reached. These checks protect different
invariants and should produce diagnostics that identify the violated
boundary.

The short-transfer backend makes progress by returning a positive count
until the request is complete or the available input ends. A real
backend must follow the same count contract. Returning more bytes than
requested is a protocol violation. Returning zero during a write-all
operation cannot complete the remaining write. Returning zero during
reading represents EOF in this interface, which is why a nonblocking
transport needs a separate policy for temporary lack of data.

The format's checksum says nothing about whether the full frame reached
its destination. Write completion and frame validation are separate
facts. Likewise, successful loading only means the bytes were obtained
under the loader's policy; it does not establish magic, version,
identifier syntax, or checksum correctness. The explicit layering lets
each test target the fact it is intended to establish.

\section{Evolving a protocol while keeping the implementation
readable}\label{c42-evolving-a-protocol-while-keeping-the-implementation-readable}

If version two adds a timestamp, choose its width, signedness, byte
order, and epoch as part of the wire specification. Writing the native
memory of a time object would import host layout and library choices
into the format. If a field becomes optional, define how absence is
represented and whether older decoders reject or skip it. A version byte
is useful only when each accepted version has a precise interpretation.

An incremental decoder can reuse the same validation sequence while
returning a richer status: complete frame, incomplete prefix, or
malformed record. It would retain enough bytes to retry after more input
arrives and publish a frame only after the trailer is validated. This is
the streaming analogue of the current cursor transaction. The bounded
buffered design is a useful starting specification because the success
and rejection behavior can be understood without transport scheduling.

Finally, keep diagnostic formatting outside the codec's core decisions.
A command-line inspector may want hexadecimal offsets, an application
may want a structured error code, and a test may want the precise
validation category. Those consumers can share one format implementation
if the error information is retained. The case study's exceptions are
deliberately small, but the staged validation and saved cursor provide
clear points for adding that information as the protocol grows.

